{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 47, :comments ["<b>Because you did not serve, etc.</b> This punishment and the one that follows it up to His saying ״אִם לֹא תִשְׁמֹר״ corresponds to the nullification of positive commandments, whose root is in the verse (Deuteronomy 28:58) ״אִם לֹא תִשְׁמֹר לַעֲשׂוֹת״, etc. And He said <b>תַּחַת אֲשֶׁר לֹא עָבַדְתָּ אֶת ה׳</b> — meaning, to perform the commandments that He commanded you to perform with joy; therefore ״וְעָבַדְתָּ אֶת אוֹיְבֶיךָ בְּרָעָב וּבְצָמָא״, etc. — you cast off the yoke of the commandment from upon yourself, [so] your enemies will put an iron yoke upon your neck. And these punishments are directed measure for measure against casting off the yoke of positive commandments. You who did not wish to exert yourself to understand and to grasp the order of the performance of the commandments — HaShem will raise up against you a nation whose language you will not understand, etc., and so all of them." "<b>And He concluded</b> by saying <b>אִם לֹא תִשְׁמֹר לַעֲשׂוֹת</b>, to inform that for the fulfillment of positive commandments He punishes, and He included all positive commandments within the commandment of fear [of HaShem], for it is a positive commandment, as Rambam wrote at the beginning of Sefer HaMadda (Hilkhot Yesodei HaTorah, chapter 2), and this is his language: the glorious Name, etc. — a commandment to fear, etc. And the reason He did not say ״אִם לֹא תַעֲשׂוּ״, I have written above (Deuteronomy 27:26) the reason that He does not obligate to do in a general sense, but rather לִשְׁמֹר לַעֲשׂוֹת — when it comes to hand and when one is able to do it." "<b>Or</b> it is possible that this verse comes to say along the lines of His saying (Leviticus 26:23) ״וְאִם בְּאֵלֶּה לֹא תִוָּסְרוּ לִי״ — meaning, that if after all that has been said he does not keep to do, etc., [out of] fear of HaShem, the glorious and awesome One — for after He has brought upon him all the curses, it will be in his eyes exceedingly awesome, for he sees that He has disciplined him. And He was precise to say <b>תִּשְׁמֹר לַעֲשׂוֹת</b>, corresponding to negative commandments and positive commandments, and He informed that HaShem will yet bring upon him extraordinary great blows and all that is said on the matter." "And He concluded by saying <b>וְנִשְׁאַרְתֶּם בִּמְתֵי מְעָט וְגוֹ׳ תַּחַת אֲשֶׁר וְגוֹ׳ כִּי לֹא שָׁמַעְתָּ בְּקוֹל ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ</b> — this corresponds to the fact that he did not do repentance for the nullification of Torah study. <b>וְהָיָה כַּאֲשֶׁר שָׂשׂ</b>, etc. — lest a person say, after we know that HaShem our Elohim — all His attributes are attributes of kindness and mercy, His attributes will prevent Him from prolonging further the troubles and His hand will slacken. Therefore He informed that joy is to Him כַּאֲשֶׁר שָׂשׂ, etc., and accordingly, nature does not grow weary of joys. And similar to this the verse said (Proverbs 11:10) ״בַּאֲבֹד רְשָׁעִים רִנָּה״. And so our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted (Sanhedrin 39a) on the verse (I Kings 22:36) ״וַתַּעֲבֹר הָרִנָּה״ — that at the destruction of Ahab son of Omri there was rejoicing before HaShem. And Yonatan translated ״יָשִׂישׂ ה׳״ — יֶחְדֵּי מֵימְרָא דַה׳ עֲלֵיכוֹן עַמְמִין נוּכְרָאִין וְכוּ׳, and he was precise [that] ״יָשִׂישׂ״ is a verb that goes out to others. Nevertheless, the plain sense of the verse is not uprooted from its place, as Onkelos translated — except that Yonatan ben Uzziel interpreted the verse [homiletically]." "<b>And I observed</b> to note: why were consolations not stated in these curses in the order stated in the curses of parashat BeHukkotai (Leviticus 26:42–44)? Also there is reason to note: why did He double the curses, and were the curses He spoke in parashat BeHukkotai not sufficient?" "<b>And it will be seen</b> that it was necessary to repeat the curses, because the curses in parashat BeChukotai were stated regarding the collectivity of Israel, for they were all stated in plural language from beginning to end. And there is room to say that if a portion of Israel improve their deeds, HaShem does not hold it against the portion that acts wickedly. For this reason the curses stated in this parashah came and were all stated in singular language, intending to speak whether of an actual individual or of a portion of Israel — even though they be many in terms of the collectivity of Israel, they are reckoned as a singular. And therefore you will find that He said <b>וְהָיוּ שָׁמֶיךָ אֲשֶׁר עַל רֹאשְׁךָ</b> etc., <b>יִשָּׂא ה׳ עָלֶיךָ גּוֹי מֵרָחוֹק</b> — this is regarding a portion of Israel; though they are many, He speaks to them in singular language for the reason we have written. And He also said ״אִשָּׁה תְאָרֵשׂ״ etc., ״בַּיִת תִּבְנֶה״ etc. — stated unambiguously in singular." "<b>And now</b> we have gained understanding of why no consolation was stated among them, for consolation was stated only regarding the curses directed at the collectivity of Israel — for He will not abandon them to annihilate them, and He will remember for them the covenant of their fathers and also remember the Land. But this is not so regarding the individuals among them who act wickedly — fire shall consume them forever. And surely you will find that HaShem commanded to destroy a city of Israel and to make it an eternal mound, and this is the Ir HaNidachat (Deuteronomy 13:14–19), and needless to say a single man who acts wickedly — to put him to death in court. And what consolation is there for this after he is destroyed? And so you will find in parashat Nitzavim, when HaShem mentioned the wickedness of a single family and tribe, He said (Deuteronomy 29:19) ״וּמָחָה ה׳ אֶת שְׁמוֹ״ etc., and did not mention His consolation. And likewise the curses stated in this parashah, since they are directed at individuals of Israel." "And let it not be difficult in your eyes that the verse spoke two or three words in this parashah in plural language, as it said <b>וְנִשְׁאַרְתֶּם מְתֵי מִסְפָּר</b>, <b>לְהַאֲבִיד אֶתְכֶם</b>, <b>וְהִתְמַכַּרְתֶּם</b> — for after He began speaking in singular language, He did not scruple to finish the utterance in plural language, since the matter is understood that He is speaking of the many among the individuals. And the proof is ״לְאֹיְבֶיךָ״ — singular language." "<b>And if</b> you say: He should have stated the curses of BeChukotai in singular language and would not have needed to repeat these curses — for I would have said that the verse does not punish with all the severe punishment except individuals, but not the collectivity of Israel. Or even if we say that their sin is great, and we learn from the punishment of individuals, we still have no guarantee of the consolation that HaShem will remember the covenant of the fathers — Scripture therefore states the curses of BeChukotai to say that the collectivity too will be punished thus, and yet they do have consolation, that HaShem will not abrogate the covenant of our fathers and will restore our fortunes." "<b>We thus conclude</b> that HaShem will punish for neglecting Torah study, and for transgressing negative commandments, and for neglecting positive commandments. And even though they said in the Gemara (Menachot 41a) that one is not punished for failure to perform a positive commandment except at a time of wrath — there are positive commandments for which one is liable, such as circumcision and Pesach. Also, one who resolves in his mind to deny positive commandments and not perform them — this one HaShem will curse and prepare him for punishment according to all that is written."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {9 "In the last sentence, the word המסכים appears to mean 'one who resolves/agrees in his mind'; rendered accordingly."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:20:19.013111288Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}